    "product_id": "81b36dc4-6cca-4d55-88c8-35d63c3c0f9f",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 042119\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n219 PM MST Sun Oct 4 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Above normal temperatures, with minimal day-to-day changes\n  through the start of the week, as afternoon highs reach 97 to\n  103 degrees across the lower deserts.\n\n- Breezy to locally windy conditions can be expected in the\n  mornings and early afternoons across south-central and \n  southwestern Arizona through Monday with gusts reaching 20-35 \n  mph.\n\n- Temperatures across southeast California and southwest Arizona\n  warm to near record-level Wednesday and Thursday.\n\n- Dry weather with zero rain chances will persist through the \n  upcoming week.\n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH TUESDAY/... \n\nDry and warm conditions continue across the region with broad high\npressure positioned over the Southwest CONUS. A strong E-W\npressure gradient remains in place across southern AZ and has\nresulted in wind gusts today up to 30-40 mph across parts of \nsouth-central AZ. Several dust channels developed and were visible\non satellite across Pinal County around midday. Magnitude of wind\ngusts are expected to wane through the afternoon and this \nevening. High temperatures this afternoon will be similar to \nyesterday for most areas and a couple degrees warmer across south-\ncentral AZ, topping out around 97-102 degrees.\n\nWeather conditions heading into Monday will once again be similar\nto today with breezy easterly winds, as the pressure gradient\npersists, and above normal temperatures with afternoon highs again\naround 97-102 degrees. Easterly wind gusts during the Monday are\nforecast to peak around 25-35 mph in the morning and then weaken\nthrough the rest of the day as the pressure gradient finally\nrelaxes with lowering surface pressure to the east. By Tuesday,\na shortwave trough, which is currently just off the CA coast, will\nmove eastward inland through NV and UT and dislodge the high\npressure over the region while also shifting mid-level flow from\nE-SE to W-NW. Despite these changes aloft, there will not be any\nnotable immediate changes at the surface. Temperatures Tuesday \nwill remain nearly unchanged at 5-10 degrees above normal with\nhighs around 97-103 degrees across the lower deserts. The surface\npressure gradient will at least relax further into Tuesday. There\nmay still be some morning easterly breezes, but overall winds will\nbe much lighter Tuesday across the region. Skies will remain\nmostly clear through the start of the workweek with no rain\nexpected\n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /WEDNESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/... \n\nBy Wednesday, a quasi-omega block is shown to develop with an\nanticyclone developing near/over the SoCal coast. Forecast \nconfidence is good that this pattern will hold through the \nend of the workweek. This will result in temperatures remaining \nfairly steady across south-central AZ with afternoon highs in the\n95-100 degree range across the lower deserts and ranging from the\nmid 80s to lower 90s across the higher terrain areas. Meanwhile\ntemperatures are expected to heat up across southeast CA and\nsouthwest AZ with a strengthening of the nearby anticyclone. \nLatest NBM forecast continues to show afternoon highs reaching the\n100-106 degree range across the lower deserts in SE CA and SW AZ \nfor both Wednesday and Thursday. These temperatures will be over \n10 degrees above normal and near daily records in both El Centro, \nCA and Yuma, AZ both days. These temperatures also will likely \nlead to Major HeatRisk for this time of year, with probability of \nMajor HeatRisk at 50-70%. As a result, an Extreme Heat Watch has \nbeen issued for the Yuma area and Imperial Valley for Wednesday \nand Thursday. With high pressure in place dry conditions are \nexpected to persist with no chances for rain, except for near the \nWhite Mtns Wednesday with a weak shortwave moving through the Four\nCorners.\n\nThe high pressure to the west is then expected to start to weaken\nby the end of the workweek and into the weekend with a trough \nmoving into western CONUS this upcoming weekend. However, forecast\nconfidence decreases considerably by the end of the weekend and\nstart of the following week as the ensemble spread is large with \nthe timing of the high weakening and moving out of the region and \nthe trough moving in. The ensembles also disagree on the strength \nof the trough for this upcoming weekend. Either way temperatures \nare expected to gradually decrease back toward normal by this \nupcoming weekend. Additionally, dry conditions look to continue \nthrough at least Saturday, but this could change depending on the\nevolution of troughing during the weekend. Right now better \nchances for rain look to arrive by the beginning of next week, \nwith the best chances across the higher terrain in eastern AZ. \nStay tuned for updates.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 1735Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT:\nThe main aviation concern will be round of breezy conditions\nduring the TAF period. Gusts today will be strongest during the\nafternoon, reaching upwards of 25-30 kt at times, but will be on a\ngradual downtrend through the day. Winds will remain relatively\nelevated overnight before gusts closer to 20-25 kt develop once\nagain late Monday morning. These enhanced winds will have the\npotential to generate some lofted dust, but surface VIS reductions\nare not expected, though slantwise concerns may arise, especially\nas we get closer to sunset. LLWS shear may also present some\noperational challenges, but should remain below TAF criteria.\nMostly clear skies will be present through the forecast window.\n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH:\nNo major aviation concerns are expected over the next 24 hours.\nWinds will be favored out of the E/NE at each terminal through\nthis afternoon before tilting back toward the NNE at KBLH and NW\nat KIPL by this evening. Clear skies will be common across the\nregion through Monday morning. \n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nExpect easterly winds to remain elevated around 8-15 mph with \nearly morning and afternoon gusts up to 20-35 mph, and locally \nhigher gusts of 35-40 mph, today mainly across SW AZ and the \neastern district. Winds will be a touch weaker Monday and then \nwinds will return to their more typical light diurnal tendencies \non Tuesday with only minor afternoon upslope gustiness. These\nlighter winds may only last a couple days before gusty easterly\ngradient winds develop again by Friday. Dry conditions with above\nnormal temperatures will continue through the upcoming week. A \nslight increase in temperatures, into the 101-106 degree range is\nanticipated across southeast CA and southwest AZ Wednesday and\nThursday. MinRHs will mostly range from 15-25% across the western\ndistricts to 20-35% across eastern districts with fair to good \novernight recovery (35-60%) area-wide. No wetting rain expected\nthrough the next 7 days.\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...Extreme Heat Watch from Wednesday morning through Thursday \n     evening for AZZ532.\n\nCA...Extreme Heat Watch from Wednesday morning through Thursday \n     evening for CAZ566-567.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Benedict\nLONG TERM...Benedict/Berislavich \nAVIATION...RW \nFIRE WEATHER...Benedict/Berislavich\n",
